    "result_text": "The build is green. Let me summarize what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test run, 0 failures). The `AcceptanceTest.coreNoBulkWarningUnderThreshold` test now sees `$.bulkSignupWarning` = `false` on a fresh create.\n\n### The change\n\nPer the spec, when **more than 80** owners have already been created for a given day, the newly created owner's response now carries `bulkSignupWarning: true`; otherwise `false`. I followed the existing `namesakeCount` pattern exactly \u2014 a value computed at creation time, persisted, and read back on later `GET`s \u2014 since the acceptance test reads the field via `GET /api/owners/{id}` after creating.\n\nFiles touched:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `bulkSignupWarning` boolean, read-only property to the `Owner` schema. This drives the generated `OwnerDto` (getter/setter confirmed generated).\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `Boolean bulkSignupWarning` field (`@Column(name = \"bulk_signup_warning\")`) with getter/setter. MapStruct auto-wired it in both directions (verified in `OwnerMapperImpl`).\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 in `addOwner`, set `owner.setBulkSignupWarning(isBulkSignupDay(owner.getRegistrationDate()))` before saving. The new helper counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` matches the new owner's date (the same accumulation path as the daily-limit rule) and returns `true` when that count exceeds the `BULK_SIGNUP_WARNING_THRESHOLD` of 80.\n\n4. **Schema files** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 added the `bulk_signup_warning` column to the `owners` table.\n\nNote the threshold (>80) sits below the existing daily hard limit (`MAX_OWNERS_PER_DAY = 100`), so the warning flags owners 82\u2013100 while creation is still permitted, then the daily-limit rule rejects at 100.",
